two guards accused of not checking on  Jeffrey Epstein the day he died in New  York have pleaded not guilty to charges  of falsifying records and conspiracy  they allegedly did not check on him for  about eight hours mullah legi is outside  the Manhattan Correctional Facility  where Epstein died mola  why such serious charges well good  morning Anthony no Jeffrey Epstein was  kept at the special housing unit here at  the MCC where guards were supposed to  check on him every 30 minutes  prosecutors say that didn't happen but  one of EPs teens criminal defense  attorneys as well as the union that  represents these correctional officers  argue that these guards are being made  scapegoats according to a federal  indictment correctional officer Tobin  Noel logged more than 75 inmate counts  the day Jeffrey Epstein died but  evidence allegedly shows that she and  fellow officer Michael Thomas did not  perform any of their required rounds  Thomas's attorney blames systemic  failures in the Bureau of Prisons we  believe that when all the evidence is  presented about how the system was ran  maybe you'll have a different  appreciation for what was going on  Epstein was found unresponsive in his  cell with a noose around his neck around  6:30 a.m. on August 10th  he'd attempted suicide just weeks  earlier we take every inmates life very  seriously in the Bureau of Prisons on  Capitol Hill Tuesday senators grilled  the Bureau of Prisons director about the  failure to protect Epstein there's got  to be their major malfunction of the  system or criminal enterprise at foot to  allow this to happen so are you looking  at both is the FBI looking at both if  the FBI is involved then they are  looking at criminal enterprise yes  surveillance video allegedly shows that  aside from Noel Thomas and two other  officers at the facility no one entered  the section where Epstein was held  during that shift prosecutors say in the  hours before  Epstein's body was discovered the  officers were seen sitting in a common  area approximately 15 feet from his cell  the indictment claims they spent that  time surfing the internet and for about  two hours both appeared to have been  asleep CBS News legal analyst Ricky  Clayman the purpose of those tapes  is to put an end to conspiracy theories  and show that no one else had access to  Jeffrey Epstein at the time of his death  both guards are now out on $100,000 bond  Thomas's attorney says they are looking  forward to their day in court hoping for  a positive resolution both guards are  expected back in court next Monday Tony  and we'll be watching more thank you  very much
 

